To create a Chrome extension that saves the contents of the current webpage, you can follow these steps:

1. **Set up your project structure:**
   - Create a new directory for your extension.
   - Inside this directory, create two files: `manifest.json` and `content.js`.

2. **Create the `manifest.json` file:**
   This file contains metadata about your extension and specifies its permissions and background scripts.

```json
{
  "manifest_version": 3,
  "name": "Save Page Content",
  "version": "1.0",
  "description": "Saves the content of the current webpage.",
  "permissions": [
    "activeTab",
    "storage"
  ],
  "background": {
    "service_worker": "background.js"
  },
  "action": {
    "default_popup": "popup.html",
    "default_icon": {
      "16": "icons/icon16.png",
      "48": "icons/icon48.png",
      "128": "icons/icon128.png"
    }
  },
  "icons": {
    "16": "icons/icon16.png",
    "48": "icons/icon48.png",
    "128": "icons/icon128.png"
  }
}
```

3. **Create the `content.js` file:**
   This script will be injected into the web pages to extract their content.

```javascript
// content.js

function savePageContent() {
  const body = document.body;
  const html = body.innerHTML;

  // Send the HTML content back to the background script
  chrome.runtime.sendMessage({ action: 'saveContent', data: html });
}

// Listen for messages from the popup
chrome.runtime.onMessage.addListener((message) => {
  if (message.action === 'save') {
    savePageContent();
  }
});
```

4. **Create the `background.js` file:**
   This script handles saving the content to local storage or another location.

```javascript
// background.js

chrome.runtime.onMessage.addListener((message, sender, sendResponse) => {
  if (message.action === 'saveContent') {
    // Save the content to local storage
    localStorage.setItem('savedContent', message.data);

    // Notify the user
    chrome.action.setBadgeText({ text: 'Saved' });
    setTimeout(() => {
      chrome.action.setBadgeText({ text: '' });
    }, 5000);
  }
});
```

5. **Create the `popup.html` file:**
   This is the UI that users interact with when they click on the extension icon.

```html
<!-- popup.html -->

<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8">
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
  <title>Save Page Content</title>
  <style>
    body {
      font-family: Arial, sans-serif;
      padding: 10px;
    }
    button {
      margin-top: 10px;
      padding: 10px 20px;
      cursor: pointer;
    }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
  <h1>Save Page Content</h1>
  <button id="saveButton">Save Current Page</button>

  <script src="popup.js"></script>
</body>
</html>
```

6. **Create the `popup.js` file:**
   This script adds functionality to the popup, such as handling the save button click event.

```javascript
// popup.js

document.getElementById('saveButton').addEventListener('click', () => {
  chrome.tabs.query({ active: true, currentWindow: true }, (tabs) => {
    chrome.scripting.executeScript({
      target: { tabId: tabs[0].id },
      function: () => {
        window.postMessage({ type: 'save' }, '*');
      }
    });
  });
});
```

7. **Add icons:**
   You need to add some icons for your extension. Place them in an `icons` folder within your project directory.

8. **Load the extension in Chrome:**
   - Open Chrome and go to `chrome://extensions/`.
   - Enable "Developer mode".
   - Click "Load unpacked" and select the root directory of your extension.

Now, when you visit any webpage and click on the extension icon, it should prompt you to save the current page's content. The content will then be saved locally using the browser's storage API.

