Sure, I can help you with that! The Strategy Pattern is a behavioral design pattern that enables selecting an algorithm at runtime. In the context of a banking application, we could have different strategies for calculating interest on savings accounts.

Here's a simple example in JavaScript:

```javascript
// Define the interface for all strategies
class InterestStrategy {
  calculateInterest(balance) {
    throw new Error("This method should be overridden by subclasses");
  }
}

// Concrete strategy: Simple Interest
class SimpleInterest extends InterestStrategy {
  calculateInterest(balance) {
    return balance * 0.05; // 5% simple interest
  }
}

// Concrete strategy: Compound Interest
class CompoundInterest extends InterestStrategy {
  calculateInterest(balance) {
    const rate = 0.06; // 6% compound interest
    const years = 1;
    return balance * Math.pow(1 + rate, years) - balance;
  }
}

// Context class that uses the strategy
class BankAccount {
  constructor(strategy) {
    this.strategy = strategy;
  }

  setStrategy(strategy) {
    this.strategy = strategy;
  }

  getBalance() {
    return this.balance;
  }

  setBalance(balance) {
    this.balance = balance;
  }

  applyInterest() {
    if (this.strategy instanceof InterestStrategy) {
      this.balance += this.strategy.calculateInterest(this.balance);
    } else {
      throw new Error("Invalid strategy provided");
    }
  }
}

// Usage
const account = new BankAccount(new SimpleInterest());
account.setBalance(1000);

console.log(`Initial Balance: $${account.getBalance()}`);
account.applyInterest();
console.log(`After applying simple interest: $${account.getBalance()}`);

account.setStrategy(new CompoundInterest());
account.applyInterest();
console.log(`After applying compound interest: $${account.getBalance()}`);
```

In this example:
- `InterestStrategy` is the base class defining the contract for interest calculation.
- `SimpleInterest` and `CompoundInterest` are concrete implementations of the `InterestStrategy`.
- `BankAccount` is the context class that holds a reference to the current interest strategy and applies it when needed.

This way, you can easily switch between different interest calculation methods without changing the core logic of your banking application.

