Certainly! Below is a MATLAB LiveScript that simulates the generation of water waves due to a fan blowing over a water tank. This simulation uses basic fluid dynamics principles and assumes linear wave theory for simplicity.

```matlab
% Water Tank Dimensions
L = 0.42; % Length (m)
W = 0.36; % Width (m)

% Wind Speed
U = 2.5; % m/s

% Time Parameters
tspan = [0 10]; % Simulation time from 0 to 10 seconds
dt = 0.01; % Time step
t = tspan(1):dt:tspan(2);

% Spatial Grid
x = linspace(-L/2, L/2, 100); % x-coordinates
y = linspace(-W/2, W/2, 100); % y-coordinates
[X, Y] = meshgrid(x, y);

% Initial Conditions
eta = zeros(size(X)); % Surface elevation

% Wave Number
k = 2*pi/L;

% Angular Frequency
omega = sqrt(g*k*tanh(k*H));

% Function to calculate surface elevation
function eta = calculate_eta(U, k, omega, X, Y, t)
    % Assuming a simple sinusoidal wave form
    eta = U * (1 - cos(k*X + omega*t));
end

% Main Loop
for i = 1:length(t)
    eta(:,:,i) = calculate_eta(U, k, omega, X, Y, t(i));
end

% Plotting the Results
figure;
surf(X, Y, squeeze(eta(:,:,end)));
xlabel('X Position (m)');
ylabel('Y Position (m)');
zlabel('Surface Elevation (m)');
title('Water Waves Generated by Fan');
colorbar;
shading interp;
axis tight;
view(3);
```

